# Storybook Stories Rule

Components need storybook stories that demonstrate their functionality and variants.

## Story Location and Naming (CRITICAL)

### ⚠️ IMPORTANT: Always Use Correct Title Format
**ALL STORY TITLES MUST START WITH `Spectrum/Components/`**

- Stories should be placed in `packages/storybook/src/stories/components/{component-name}`
- The path should mirror the component's location in the core package
- For example:
  - Component: `packages/core/src/components/spectrum-button/spectrum-button.tsx`
  - Story: `packages/storybook/src/stories/components/spectrum-button/spectrum-button.stories.tsx`

### 🔴 MANDATORY Title Formats:
- **Main Story File**: `title: 'Spectrum/Components/SpectrumComponentName'`
- **Dependencies MDX**: `<Meta title="Spectrum/Components/SpectrumComponentName/Dependencies" />`
- **Use Cases MDX**: `<Meta title="Spectrum/Components/SpectrumComponentName/Use Cases" />`

### ✅ Before Creating Stories:
1. **CHECK EXISTING PATTERN**: Look at SpectrumBadge, SpectrumButton stories in Storybook navigation
2. **VERIFY PREFIX**: All titles must start with "Spectrum/Components/"
3. **CONFIRM LOCATION**: Stories appear under Spectrum > Components in navigation

### ❌ Common Mistakes to Avoid:
- **WRONG**: `title: 'Components/SpectrumComponentName'` (missing Spectrum prefix)
- **WRONG**: `<Meta title="Components/SpectrumComponentName/Dependencies" />` (missing Spectrum prefix)

- Stories should be grouped in alphabetical order
- If there is deeper structure in Core, this should be reflected in Storybook with nested folders

## Story Requirements

### Core Story Structure
Each component should have a comprehensive story ecosystem with the following files:

#### 1. Main Story File (`component-name.stories.ts`)
- **Comprehensive TypeScript story** with proper types and controls
- **Component documentation** with basic usage examples
- **Story categories**: Playground, Basic Examples, Variant Showcase, Feature Demonstrations, Real-World Usage Examples
- **All stories prefixed with "Spectrum"** (e.g., SpectrumPrimary, SpectrumAllVariants)
- **Organized import structure** from Examples folder
- **Proper argTypes** with descriptions and default values

#### 2. Examples Folder (`Examples/`)
Organized example files for maintainable story structure:
- **BasicExamples.ts** - Core button configurations and fundamental usage
- **VariantExamples.ts** - All variant showcases (primary, secondary, success, etc.)
- **FeatureExamples.ts** - Interactive features (sound, haptic, ripple, icons, sizes, animations)
- **UsageExamples.ts** - Real-world patterns (forms, navigation, media controls, toolbars, CTAs, loading states, accessibility)

#### 3. Dependencies MDX (`component-name-dependencies.mdx`)
- **Meta title format**: `<Meta title="Spectrum/Components/ComponentName/Dependencies" />`
- **Visual mermaid diagram** showing component relationships
- **Dependency documentation** with integration patterns
- **Usage examples** in dependent components
- **Architecture context** explaining component's role in the system

#### 4. Use Cases MDX (`component-name-use-cases.mdx`)
- **Meta title format**: `<Meta title="Spectrum/Components/ComponentName/Use Cases" />`
- **Real-world scenarios** organized by category
- **Complete code examples** for each use case
- **Best practices** for different contexts
- **Business application examples** (e-commerce, dashboards, content creation)
- **Accessibility and mobile considerations**

#### 5. Component README (`README.md`)
- **Complete usage guidelines** and API documentation
- **Properties documentation** with examples
- **Installation and setup** instructions
- **Code examples** for common patterns

### Story Organization Pattern
```
📁 spectrum-button/
  📄 spectrum-button.stories.ts        # Main story file
  📄 spectrum-button-dependencies.mdx  # Dependency visualization
  📄 spectrum-button-use-cases.mdx     # Real-world scenarios
  📄 README.md                         # Component documentation
  📁 Examples/
    📄 BasicExamples.ts               # Core configurations
    📄 VariantExamples.ts            # All variants
    📄 FeatureExamples.ts            # Interactive features
    📄 UsageExamples.ts              # Real-world patterns
```

### Content Requirements
- **Comprehensive coverage** of all component variants, states, and features
- **TypeScript types** and proper Storybook controls for all properties
- **Mermaid diagrams** for dependency visualization (MDX only)
- **Real-world use cases** with complete code examples
- **Accessibility examples** and best practices
- **Mobile/responsive considerations** where applicable
- **Business context** examples (forms, e-commerce, dashboards, etc.)

### Mermaid Diagram Standards (Dependencies MDX)
**ALL dependency diagrams MUST follow the spectrum-button pattern exactly:**

#### ✅ Required Structure
1. **Foundation Component** → **Dependent Components** → **Application Use Cases**
2. **Three-tier hierarchy only** - no complex feature/variant branches
3. **Consistent color coding**:
   - `classDef foundation fill:#e1f5fe` (blue)
   - `classDef dependent fill:#f3e5f5` (purple) 
   - `classDef application fill:#fff3e0` (orange)

#### ✅ Correct Syntax
```mdx
<mermaid-diagram chart="graph TD
    SC[spectrum-component] --> |Direct Use| SA[spectrum-accordion]
    SC --> |Direct Use| SB[spectrum-button]
    
    SA --> |Application Use| CONTENT[Content Systems]
    SB --> |Application Use| FORMS[Form Systems]
    
    classDef foundation fill:#e1f5fe
    classDef dependent fill:#f3e5f5
    classDef application fill:#fff3e0
    
    class SC foundation
    class SA,SB dependent
    class CONTENT,FORMS application"></mermaid-diagram>
```

#### ❌ Incorrect Patterns
- **NO** markdown code blocks: ```` ```mermaid ````
- **NO** function imports: `import { createMermaidDiagram }`
- **NO** complex feature branches (variants, sound, animations, etc.)
- **NO** overly detailed dependency chains
- **NO** custom colors outside the three standard classes

#### 🎯 Reference Pattern
Always use **spectrum-button-dependencies.mdx** as the reference implementation for:
- Diagram structure and complexity level
- Color scheme and visual hierarchy  
- Content organization and documentation flow
- Integration pattern examples

